---
// src/components/HobbyIslandSection.astro
//
// New section in the `.sky-chain` run, sitting between
// AchievementIslandSection and BonusIslandSection (update the import +
// insertion in index.astro — see the usage example at the bottom of
// this comment).
//
// v2 — reworked after seeing your layered mockup. The three artworks
// (`backIslandImage`, `joyconImage`, `frontIslandImage`) are NOT three
// independent, independently-positioned islands like
// AchievementIslandSection's statue/temple — they're one single
// island illustration split across three files that only line up
// correctly when stacked directly on top of each other, back to
// front, with no relative offset between them.
//
// I confirmed this by feature-matching each of your three original
// uploads against your mockup image (AKAZE + RANSAC homography): all
// three map onto it with the SAME scale (~0.446x) and effectively
// zero relative translation — i.e. your mockup is just "take each
// full original canvas, scale it down by the same amount, lay them
// directly on top of each other." So rather than eyeballing three
// separate `top`/`left` positions (which is what v1 of this file did,
// and why the pieces looked scattered instead of interlocked), the
// three delivered images this time are all cropped from the exact
// SAME rectangle of their original canvases and share IDENTICAL
// pixel dimensions (1900x1028) — stack them with `inset: 0` and they
// land in register automatically, the same way your mockup does:
//
//   1. `backIslandImage`  (bed, first-aid kit, board game, campfire) — bottom layer
//   2. `joyconImage`      (the pink Joy-Con)                          — middle layer
//   3. `frontIslandImage` (fountain, pool, piano, palm tree, ...)     — top layer
//
// IMPORTANT for future edits: if any of these three files ever gets
// re-cropped or re-exported, it has to be cropped from that same
// shared rectangle (or just re-derived from the original uncropped
// canvas at the same scale) — cropping one independently of the
// other two will shift it relative to the others and the island will
// visibly come apart at the seams. There's no per-image `top`/`left`
// tuning in the CSS below to compensate with; alignment is baked into
// the image files themselves, not the layout.
//
// v4 — per your request, the three layers now drift INDEPENDENTLY on
// cursor proximity instead of moving as one rigid unit. Worth calling
// out plainly: this trades away the pixel-perfect interlock from v2/
// v3. At rest (cursor far away, or `prefers-reduced-motion`) all
// three still line up exactly, since every layer's drift settles back
// to `translate(0, 0)` — the seams only start to show once the cursor
// is close enough to actually push them apart. Kept the push small
// (see `data-depth` below) specifically to keep that gap subtle
// rather than letting the artwork visibly tear; turn the depth
// numbers down further (or back to identical) if it reads as too much
// once you see it live.
//
// Each layer gets its own two-level wrapper, same reasoning as v3 for
// why bob and drift can't share an element (a running CSS animation
// silently wins over a JS-set inline `transform` on the same
// property): a `.hobby-layer-bob` wrapper (own bob timing) around a
// `.hobby-layer-drift.parallax-inner` wrapper (own mouse-repel
// strength via `data-depth`). `data-depth` is read by the shared
// script below as a multiplier on the base push distance — back
// island 0.55x (subtle, reads as "further away"), Joy-Con 0.85x,
// front island 1.2x (strongest, reads as "closest to the viewer") —
// a small depth-of-field cue on top of the independence you asked
// for, rather than three identical pushes that just happen to be
// computed separately. Bob timing is also offset per layer (different
// duration + a negative `animation-delay` to start mid-cycle) so they
// don't bob in lockstep either. `joyconImage` is still optional (omit
// it and the other two layers still render/drift fine on their own).
//
// v5 — per your request the island is now A LOT bigger. The previous
// approach (v3/v4) kept the island absolutely positioned, bottom-
// anchored inside a fixed `100vh` section, with `.content` pinned to
// the top — which meant the island's size was always fighting the
// text for a slice of one fixed-height box, and capping it by
// viewport height (`min(..., 86vh)`) to avoid that fight quietly
// capped it fairly small on ordinary monitors too (that's why the
// previous bump barely read as bigger). This version removes that
// fight instead of continuing to referee it: the island is back in
// NORMAL DOCUMENT FLOW, stacked below `.content` (both centered via
// `display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center` on
// `.hobby-section`) rather than absolutely positioned over it. With
// `min-height: 100vh` (not a fixed `height`), the section is simply
// as tall as it needs to be to fit both comfortably — on most screens
// that's now taller than one viewport, so the very bottom of the
// island sits slightly below the fold, which is the expected trade
// for "a lot bigger" rather than a bug. No vh-based caps or
// short-viewport media queries are needed anymore — normal flow can't
// overlap by construction, at any viewport size, so `width` is just a
// plain `clamp()` now.
//
// Background: `hobby_island_background.webp` is a FLAT fill (sampled
// at ≈#01d4f3), close enough to identical to `--dusk-blue` (defined
// in Layout.astro; AchievementIslandSection's bottom edge and
// BonusIslandSection's top edge both already use it) that this
// section's seam-fades reuse `--dusk-blue` on BOTH edges. That means
// NEITHER Layout.astro NOR AchievementIslandSection.astro NOR
// BonusIslandSection.astro need any changes — all three already
// agree on this exact color at the seam.
//
// v6 — you swapped in your own re-exported CSP layers and the layout
// broke ("look size and position of the site completely changes").
// I checked the three files you sent: back island came in at
// 2002x1035, front island at 3192x1942, Joy-Con at 513x931 — three
// completely different aspect ratios, each unrelated to the other two
// and to the 1900x1028 shared canvas this component was built around.
//
// Root cause: CSP's "export selected layer" trims each layer to its
// own visible-content bounding box before saving. That's exactly what
// v2's top comment warned about — cropping one layer independently of
// the others destroys the relative positioning between them — except
// now it's happening on every export instead of once. Once a layer is
// trimmed, the empty space that encoded "this layer sits 400px lower
// and 200px right of that layer" is just gone from the file. There's
// no metadata recording the trim offset either (checked — none of the
// three files carry it), so there's no way for CODE to reconstruct
// where the layers should sit relative to each other after the fact.
// This genuinely can't be fixed by changing CSS/JS; it has to be fixed
// at export time, by not trimming in the first place.
//
// THE FIX (do this in Clip Studio Paint, takes effect on every future
// export, no code changes ever needed again):
//   Don't use "Export Selected Layer" — that's the command that trims.
//   Instead, for each of the three layers: hide every OTHER layer
//   (toggle their eye icons off) so only the one you want is visible,
//   then use the regular whole-image export you'd use for finished art
//   (File > Export > PNG/JPEG/... — NOT the per-layer export command).
//   That command always exports the full canvas size, transparent
//   wherever nothing's drawn — so if your canvas is 3840x2160, all
//   three exports come out 3840x2160 every time, in the same
//   coordinate system, no matter how much you keep editing the art
//   inside them. Repeat for the other two layers. Drop the three
//   resulting files in as backIslandImage/joyconImage/frontIslandImage
//   — same filenames is fine, no code change needed on your end.
//
// To make THIS FILE robust to that workflow going forward (so if your
// canvas size ever changes — e.g. you resize the CSP document — it's
// a two-number prop edit instead of another round-trip with me), the
// hardcoded `aspect-ratio: 3840 / 2079` is now driven by two new
// OPTIONAL props, `canvasWidth` / `canvasHeight`, defaulting to
// 3840 / 2160 (a plain, untrimmed canvas — no "-79" fudge, since that
// number only existed to match the old, already-cropped 1900x1028
// deliverables). If your actual CSP canvas is a different size, pass
// it in; otherwise leave the props off and the defaults apply.
//
// I can't repair the three files you just sent — the alignment info
// they'd need is already gone from the trimmed images themselves, not
// just missing from the code — so please re-export following the
// steps above and drop the new files in; everything else about this
// component (stacking, independent drift, bob, sizing) stays exactly
// as it was.
//
// v7 — added an optional `backIslandFrozenImage` layer that overlays
// the back island and periodically "reveals" itself (spreads out from
// its own center, holds for a few seconds, then withdraws back to the
// center the same way) at random intervals, fully hands-off once it's
// on the page. Three implementation notes:
//
//   1. Alignment: this overlay lives INSIDE the back island's own
//      `.hobby-layer-drift` wrapper (not a sibling slot like Joy-Con/
//      front island), stacked directly on top of `backIslandImage`
//      with `position: absolute; inset: 0`. That means it moves and
//      bobs together with the back island as one piece (correct — it's
//      frost ON the island, not a separate floating object), and it
//      only lines up correctly if it's exported at the exact same
//      canvas size as the rest (see v6) — confirmed your upload is
//      3840x2160, matching.
//
//   2. The "spreads out from the center, holds, withdraws" motion is a
//      `clip-path: circle()` reveal, not a scale/opacity transform —
//      animating the circle's radius from 0% to comfortably past the
//      image's farthest corner (150%, overshooting on purpose so the
//      exact aspect ratio doesn't matter) and back reads as the frost
//      actually spreading across the existing artwork, rather than the
//      whole overlay image growing/fading in place.
//
//   3. "From time to time (randomly)" is handled entirely in the
//      script below with `setTimeout` + `Math.random()` — no fixed
//      loop/interval, so appearances don't fall into a predictable
//      rhythm. Each cycle: wait a random 8-22s, play the ~6.5s reveal-
//      hold-withdraw animation once, then pick a new random wait and
//      repeat, forever, starting from when the page loads. Skipped
//      entirely under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, consistent
//      with how the bob/drift are already handled elsewhere in this
//      file. Omit the prop and nothing changes — no overlay element is
//      even rendered.
//
// Carries `.sky-chain` (collapses the gap on both sides) and
// `.halftone-hover` (opts into the single page-wide cursor-spotlight
// element from Layout.astro) — same as every other section in the
// chain, no extra markup/script needed for either.
//
// Usage — insert in src/pages/index.astro between
// AchievementIslandSection and BonusIslandSection:
//
//   import HobbyIslandSection from '../components/HobbyIslandSection.astro';
//
//   <HobbyIslandSection
//     id="section-hobbies"
//     eyebrow="Island 05"
//     title="Ut Labore Et Dolore"
//     text="Sed ut perspiciatis unde omnis iste natus error sit voluptatem accusantium doloremque laudantium, totam rem aperiam."
//     backIslandImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_back_island.webp"
//     joyconImage="/personal-cv-page/images/joycon.webp"
//     frontIslandImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_front_island.webp"
//     backgroundImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_island_background.webp"
//     backIslandFrozenImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_back_island_frozen.webp"
//     canvasWidth={3840}
//     canvasHeight={2160}
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  backIslandImage: string;
  joyconImage?: string;
  frontIslandImage: string;
  backgroundImage: string;
  /** Optional overlay that periodically reveals itself on top of the
   *  back island (see the v7 comment above). Must be exported at the
   *  same canvas size as the other layers to line up. Omit it and no
   *  overlay element is rendered at all. */
  backIslandFrozenImage?: string;
  /** Your CSP document's canvas size in px — every layer export must
   *  share this exact size (see the v6 comment above). Defaults to
   *  3840x2160; override only if your actual CSP canvas differs. */
  canvasWidth?: number;
  canvasHeight?: number;
}

const {
  id,
  eyebrow,
  title,
  text,
  backIslandImage,
  joyconImage,
  frontIslandImage,
  backgroundImage,
  backIslandFrozenImage,
  canvasWidth = 3840,
  canvasHeight = 2160,
} = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="hobby-section sky-chain seam-fade-top seam-fade-bottom halftone-hover"
  id={id}
  style={`background-image: url(${backgroundImage});`}
>
  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    <p class="body-text">{text}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="hobby-island-group" aria-hidden="true">
    <div
      class="hobby-island-group-inner"
      style={`aspect-ratio: ${canvasWidth} / ${canvasHeight};`}
    >
      <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-back">
        <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-back">
          <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="0.55">
            <img class="hobby-layer" src={backIslandImage} alt="" />
            {backIslandFrozenImage && (
              <img
                class="hobby-layer hobby-frozen-overlay"
                src={backIslandFrozenImage}
                alt=""
                data-frozen-overlay
              />
            )}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {joyconImage && (
        <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-joycon">
          <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-joycon">
            <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="0.85">
              <img class="hobby-layer" src={joyconImage} alt="" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      )}

      <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-front">
        <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-front">
          <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="1.2">
            <img class="hobby-layer" src={frontIslandImage} alt="" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-free mouse-repel drift as the rest of this family
  // (see AchievementIslandSection.astro / ComputerIslandSection.astro
  // for the original) — every `.parallax-inner` element on the page
  // eases toward a small offset away from the cursor, falling off
  // with distance. Copied verbatim rather than shared, matching how
  // every other section in this family ships its own copy. Extended
  // with one thing the original didn't need: an optional `data-depth`
  // attribute (see the markup above) that scales `maxOffset` per
  // element, so the three layers here each get a different push
  // strength instead of all three computing the exact same offset —
  // reading `data-depth` and defaulting to `1` when it's absent means
  // this still behaves identically to the original for every plain
  // `.parallax-inner` element elsewhere on the page.
  const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
    "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
  ).matches;

  if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
    const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
    const ease = 0.08;

    let mouseX = 0;
    let mouseY = 0;
    let hasMouse = false;

    const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
      el,
      depth: parseFloat(el.dataset.depth ?? "1"),
      currentX: 0,
      currentY: 0,
    }));

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      mouseX = e.clientX;
      mouseY = e.clientY;
      hasMouse = true;
    });

    function tick() {
      for (const s of state) {
        let targetX = 0;
        let targetY = 0;

        if (hasMouse) {
          const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
          const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
          const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

          const dx = restX - mouseX;
          const dy = restY - mouseY;
          const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

          if (dist < influenceRadius) {
            const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
            const nx = dx / dist;
            const ny = dy / dist;
            targetX = nx * maxOffset * s.depth * falloff;
            targetY = ny * maxOffset * s.depth * falloff;
          }
        }

        s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
        s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
        s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
      }
      requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  // Random "frozen" reveal on the back island (see the v7 comment at
  // the top of the file). Self-contained: no-ops entirely if the
  // overlay prop wasn't passed (no `[data-frozen-overlay]` element on
  // the page) or under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.
  const frozenOverlay = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
    "[data-frozen-overlay]"
  );

  if (frozenOverlay && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    const minDelay = 8000; // ms, shortest gap between appearances
    const maxDelay = 12000; // ms, longest gap between appearances

    const scheduleNext = () => {
      const delay = minDelay + Math.random() * (maxDelay - minDelay);
      setTimeout(() => {
        frozenOverlay.classList.add("is-active");
      }, delay);
    };

    // `animationend` fires once the reveal-hold-withdraw cycle
    // finishes (back at an invisible `circle(0%)` state) — remove the
    // class so `.is-active` can be re-added later to replay it, then
    // roll the next random wait.
    frozenOverlay.addEventListener("animationend", () => {
      frozenOverlay.classList.remove("is-active");
      scheduleNext();
    });

    scheduleNext();
  }
</script>

<style>
  .hobby-section {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `overflow: hidden` here — see the comment on `overflow-x:
       hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal
       clipping page-wide, so the island group is free to sit taller
       than this section's own box without being hard-clipped. */
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: top;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    /* Seam-blend targets (see the shared comment in Layout.astro).
       Both edges target `--dusk-blue` — see the top-of-file comment
       above for why that's correct even though this section sits
       between two different neighbors: the actual background is a
       flat fill equal to that color, so it already matches
       AchievementIslandSection's bottom on this section's top edge,
       and already matches BonusIslandSection's top on this section's
       bottom edge, with no new variable needed. */
    --seam-target-top: var(--dusk-blue);
    --seam-target-bottom: var(--dusk-blue);
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .body-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The whole island — one graphic made of 3 stacked layers, sized
     and positioned as a single unit. Normal flow (not absolutely
     positioned — see the v5 note at the top of the file), centered
     horizontally via the section's own `align-items: center`, with a
     little breathing room above it separating it from `.content`.
     `clamp()` keeps it readable on narrow screens without letting it
     dwarf the viewport on very wide ones — there's no viewport-HEIGHT
     term anywhere in this rule anymore, on purpose: normal flow means
     there's nothing below `.content` for the island to collide with,
     so nothing here needs to reason about how tall the viewport is. */
  .hobby-island-group {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: clamp(700px, 82vw, 1600px);
    max-width: 100%;
    margin: clamp(1.5rem, 5vh, 4rem) auto 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Fixes the group's proportions to your CSP canvas size (passed in
     via the `canvasWidth`/`canvasHeight` props, inline `style` above —
     see the v6 top-of-file comment) so every layer slot below always
     has a well-defined box to fill with `inset: 0`, regardless of how
     `width` above resolves at a given viewport size. All three
     `.hobby-layer-slot`s share this exact box at rest — it's only the
     drift/bob transforms further in that ever move a layer away from
     it. The `aspect-ratio: 3840 / 2160` here is just a fallback for
     the (non-Astro) preview harness / in case the inline style is ever
     stripped — the props above are the real source of truth. */
  .hobby-island-group-inner {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 3840 / 2160;
  }

  /* One slot per layer, all stacked on the exact same box via
     `inset: 0`. Stacking order is plain DOM order (back island first,
     Joy-Con second, front island last), so no explicit `z-index` is
     needed between them. */
  .hobby-layer-slot {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* Bob loop lives on ITS OWN wrapper, one level up from the
     `.parallax-inner` element inside it — NOT on the same element as
     the mouse-repel drift. Both a CSS animation and the JS drift
     script ultimately set `transform` on whatever element they're
     attached to, and a running CSS animation wins that fight every
     frame (animations take precedence over inline styles for the
     animated property), which would silently make the JS-driven
     translate invisible even though the script keeps computing and
     setting it. Splitting them onto two nested elements — this one
     bobs via keyframes, the child below drifts via JS — is exactly
     how AchievementIslandSection avoids the same collision (its bob
     is on `.achievement-island-left`/`-right`, the drift is on the
     nested `.achievement-island-inner`). Each layer gets its own
     duration + a negative `animation-delay` (starts the loop already
     partway through) so the three don't bob in lockstep. */
  .hobby-layer-bob-back {
    animation: hobby-bob 6.4s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  .hobby-layer-bob-joycon {
    animation: hobby-bob-joycon 5s ease-in-out -1.5s infinite;
  }

  .hobby-layer-bob-front {
    animation: hobby-bob 7.2s ease-in-out -3s infinite;
  }

  /* `.hobby-layer-bob-*` and `.hobby-layer-drift` are plain block
     divs with no explicit height — that's deliberate. Block elements
     already fill their parent's width by default, and each image's
     own intrinsic aspect ratio (all three source files share the same
     untrimmed CSP canvas size — see the v6 comment on `canvasWidth`/
     `canvasHeight` at the top of the file — matching
     `.hobby-island-group-inner`'s `aspect-ratio` above) takes care of
     height on its own once width is set — explicit height would just
     be indeterminate/ignored here anyway, since none of these three
     elements have a definite height to resolve a percentage against.
     `.hobby-layer-drift` is the mouse-repel target — see the
     `data-depth` attribute in the markup and the script above for how
     the three layers get different push strengths instead of moving
     as one unit. */
  .hobby-layer-bob-back,
  .hobby-layer-bob-joycon,
  .hobby-layer-bob-front,
  .hobby-layer-drift {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .hobby-layer-drift {
    position: relative;
  }

  .hobby-layer {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
  }

  /* Sits on top of `backIslandImage` inside the SAME
     `.hobby-layer-drift` (see the v7 comment) via `position: absolute`
     — the sibling `backIslandImage` stays in normal flow and is what
     actually gives `.hobby-layer-drift` its height, so this overlay's
     `inset: 0` box always matches it exactly. Starts fully clipped
     down to a point at its own center; `.is-active` (toggled by the
     script above) plays the reveal-hold-withdraw animation once and
     then gets removed again so it can replay later. */
  .hobby-frozen-overlay {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    clip-path: circle(0% at 50% 50%);
  }

  .hobby-frozen-overlay.is-active {
    animation: hobby-frozen-reveal 6.5s ease-in-out;
  }

  /* 150% deliberately overshoots — for a `circle()` radius without a
     `farthest-corner` keyword, 100% doesn't reliably reach the corners
     of a non-square box, and overshooting costs nothing here since a
     circle bigger than the image just means "fully visible," same as
     any other value past full coverage. Holds fully visible from ~18%
     to ~72% of the 6.5s duration (a little over 3.5s) before
     withdrawing back to the same center point it grew from. */
  @keyframes hobby-frozen-reveal {
    0% { clip-path: circle(0% at 50% 50%); }
    18% { clip-path: circle(150% at 50% 50%); }
    72% { clip-path: circle(150% at 50% 50%); }
    100% { clip-path: circle(0% at 50% 50%); }
  }

  @keyframes hobby-bob {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-10px); }
  }

  /* Joy-Con gets a little extra character (a slight rotate along with
     the bob) since it's independent again anyway — same idea as the
     standalone accent treatment from v1, before it became one rigid
     piece with the islands in v2/v3. */
